[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Every year, some company claims they\'ve cracked long duration energy storage, and every year, we\'re still leaning on lithium-ion batteries that top out after a few hours. You\'ve heard the promises, you\'ve seen the press releases, and you\'re probably as tired of the hype as I am. But in January of 2026, a small'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'California startup called Noon Energy did something that really caught my attention. They demonstrated over 100 hours of stored energy from a single system the size of a shipping container. Not in a lab, not in a simulation, a'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'real working system. Now, 100 hours is a big deal. Most lithium-ion batteries give you somewhere between 2 and 10. That gap is a big reason why it\'s so hard to quit fossil fuels. We generate plenty of renewable energy, we just'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can\'t hold onto it long enough. Energy storage is one of Earth\'s biggest challenges, [music] but ironically, Noon started on another planet entirely. The core technology behind their system was originally designed to make oxygen [music]'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on Mars. Yes, that Mars, the dusty red guy up there with wonky moons. Noon\'s co-founder and CEO, Chris Graves, [music] is a former NASA engineer. He helped develop the tech to split CO2 into carbon and oxygen on another planet. His company started when he'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'realized he could use that same [music] process to store energy here on Earth. 100 hours of energy storage, that\'s a big claim, and I\'ve been burned by claims before. So, let\'s dig into what Noon is actually doing and its Mars'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connection, and whether this thing has a real shot at changing the energy storage game. Oddly enough, this whole story &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; might be best explained by something I absolutely did not pay enough attention to in high school biology. I\'m Matt Ferrell. Welcome to Undecided.'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; This video is brought to you by Incogni. Okay, so we know the problem. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Typical grid-scale lithium-ion energy storage tops out at around a few hours. Renewables generate more than enough'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'power, we just lose it. But here\'s the part that doesn\'t get talked about enough. It\'s not just the inconvenience, it\'s the failure point. It\'s the failure point for making renewables a bigger part of the grid, because when the sun goes down or the wind dies for a few'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'days, people don\'t stop demanding energy. And this leaves grid operators with exactly one option: to fire up natural gas peaker plants and crank out that dirty, expensive energy like it\'s 1899. The bottleneck isn\'t generation, it\'s'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'duration. We need storage that can ride out days, not hours. And that\'s a fundamentally different engineering problem than making a better lithium-ion cell. This is why Noon\'s 100-hour demo matters. It\'s not just a bigger battery,'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s a completely different approach to the problem. This is the part where high school Matt would have checked out. And before we get to the part where teenage Matt wasn\'t listening, let\'s look at the Mars tech because Noon\'s'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'blueprint for grid-scale renewable storage started with a NASA project to keep astronauts alive. That project is called MOXIE, which stands for Mars oxygen in situ resource utilization experiment.'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; Say that three times fast and you\'ll be reminded just how important oxygen is. Simply put, MOXIE makes oxygen from an atmosphere that doesn\'t have any. In 2020, the tech was sent to Mars on the Perseverance rover and it was the first'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of its kind to make it to another planet, which [music] is just really cool. You see, NASA would love to send manned missions to Mars, but they also want to make sure that they can offer astronauts more than a one-way ticket. Getting spacecraft [music] and astronauts back'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from Mars safely is a gigantic challenge. The challenge starts with fuel and fuel requires oxygen. You need a lot of it to get off the big red rock, more than you can bring with you. It would take roughly [music] 500 tons of oxygen from Earth to propel a manned'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'craft off of Mars. You\'re definitely not getting free two-day shipping on that. Not even on Prime Day. So, you can\'t bring oxygen to Mars and you also can\'t find it there. The atmosphere is inconveniently 95% carbon dioxide. This is a tough situation if'

[03:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re sitting at Mission Control. You just can\'t let Matt Damon die. I\'m not going to die. So, then the question to ask is, [music] what if there was a way to make oxygen on Mars using all that CO2 in the atmosphere? Think I\'m having flashbacks to freshman'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'biology again. Some piece of knowledge is implanted [music] deep within me. That\'s right. MOXIE did something similar to photosynthesis. Just like your tree in your backyard, it gobbled up CO2 in the atmosphere and'

[04:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'split it into carbon and oxygen. It then released the leftover carbon back into the atmosphere and analyzed the purity of the oxygen it created, which was then released as well. Now, if MOXIE could only make sugar, too, I could plant it'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in my backyard. MOXIE effectively produced oxygen seven times between April and November of 2021. If scaled, this technology could produce enough oxygen to launch a manned vehicle in around 26 months. It\'s definitely not a fast solution, but'

[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'oxygen on Mars is still oxygen on Mars. But, how does this tie back to Earth? We\'ll get to that, but first, MOXIE\'s whole job is pulling something valuable out of a hostile environment. Today\'s sponsor, Incogni, does the same thing'

[04:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right here on Earth. They help you pull your information back out of the hands of data brokers who collect and sell your details to people who might want to use it against you. We\'re talking about real threats, like identity theft, scams, and stalking, because they can\'t'

[04:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'harm you if they can\'t find you. Your home address, phone number, and relatives\' information are all being sold online, and the criminals use this data to take out fraudulent loans or track people down in real life. I signed up for Incogni, gave them the legal'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right to work on my behalf, and I just sat back and relaxed. It\'s really that easy. Their customer removals feature is really cool. You can flag any website where your data is exposed, and they\'ll get it removed. I\'ve seen a dramatic drop in where my information is showing'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up online. If you want to take back control of your personal information, give Incogni a try. Use the code Undecided at the link below and get 60% off an annual plan. Thanks to Incogni and to all of you for supporting the channel. All right, so'

[05:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'let\'s get back to how this ties back to Earth. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Chris Graves realized that this Martian technology could be a game-changer on Earth, too. That\'s why he co-founded Noon Energy in 2018 and serves as its CEO.'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Graves knows that storage is critical for achieving 100% reliable renewable energy systems. I agree. Until we get reliable storage, we\'re stuck with fossil fuels. Let\'s bring all of this down to Earth, so to speak. How does this actually work? As you might have'

[05:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'guessed, Noon uses a process that\'s a lot like photosynthesis. The system captures CO2 from the atmosphere and splits it into carbon and oxygen. But here\'s where the process is more like that half-dead philodendron in your'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dining room and less like Moxi. The carbon is used in solid form to actually store the energy within the battery, while the oxygen is released back into the atmosphere. Yes, just like photosynthesis, the carbon is used to store energy, though'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not in sugar form. While the oxygen is released as a byproduct. However, unlike a plant, the carbon is then oxidized, releasing the chemical energy. Oxygen is first ionized at the cathode side and'

[06:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then recombined with the carbon on the anode side of the solid oxide fuel cell. Electrons that are released have to flow through wires in a return path to the oxygen side of the fuel cell. In other words, electricity.'

[06:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Using carbon as the energy storage medium for these batteries &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; means there\'s no production constraints from materials. This is the big difference from lithium-ion batteries. In fact, Noon Energy\'s ultra-long duration energy'

[06:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'storage [music] uses less than 1% of the critical materials needed for lithium-ion batteries. Just 1%. Noon systems don\'t cost much after installations, and the company hopes they can be easily scaled. They have'

[07:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'three main components: the power block that converts between electricity and stored energy, the charge [music] tank that converts excess energy into carbon-based media storage, and the discharge tank that converts the stored energy back into electricity to be used.'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And what sets Noon apart is the separation of the power blocks from the charge tanks. This means sizing for more storage doesn\'t require duplication of other components, making scaling far more affordable. It\'s a similar scaling'

[07:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'benefit as redox flow batteries that I\'ve covered before on the channel. And when I say affordable, are we talking about Bentley is more affordable compared to a Rolls-Royce or is it actually affordable affordable? Well, if their initial data tells us'

[07:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anything, I mean actually affordable. The levelized cost of storage or LCOS for 100 MW of lithium-ion or 100 hour storage is around $1.20 per kWh. The LCOS for Noon\'s ultra-long duration energy'

[08:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'storage is around 5 cents per kWh. Noon claims that their system could enable grid-scale solar power for less than fossil fuel generation. And if it\'s true, that\'s huge. Even better, Grave says they can do it with a smaller'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'footprint than other battery systems with 1/3 the volume and mass of lithium-ion batteries and 50 times less than flow batteries. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; So, if you\'re asking yourself, wait, you\'re telling me this battery lasts up'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to 10 times longer than the best conventional lithium-ion batteries, takes up less space, is cheaper, pulls carbon dioxide out of the atmosphere, and releases oxygen back into the atmosphere?'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Well, sort of. Noon is, in the end, more of a battery plant than a plant plant. In photosynthesis, a plant takes in water, sunlight, and CO2, which it splits up, releasing the oxygen and using that carbon to make sugar, which'

[08:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is just plant fuel. The carbon that Noon takes in is released back into the atmosphere when the energy in the battery is used. But there is no net release of carbon. It only releases what it took in to begin with. It\'s a step up'

[09:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from burning fossil fuels, even if it doesn\'t reduce the total amount of CO2 in the atmosphere. There are also other factors to consider. While Noon has done some large-scale testing with positive results, this is still a relatively new'

[09:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'technology that will need time to prove it has the capacity to revolutionize the renewable energy game. In fact, Noon doesn\'t advocate for the complete replacement of lithium-ion batteries. They believe that their ultra-long'

[09:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'duration energy storage could be best served as long-term storage that feeds into the current lithium-ion batteries for immediate use. There\'s also the question around how efficient the system is compared to lithium-ion\'s round-trip efficiency.'

[09:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There are various reports showing this system is somewhere between 60 and 80% efficient, which is much lower than the 85 to 95% efficiency of lithium-ion batteries. But I\'d argue that high efficiency is not as important for'

[09:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ultra-long duration energy storage. This is much more comparable to something like pumped hydro energy storage and around 70 to 80% efficiency. If the installation and operational costs of Noon\'s system are as low as they'

[10:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'promise, the efficiency question becomes less of an issue because of just how cheap it is. So, is Noon\'s tech the breakthrough we need to finally break up with fossil fuels or just more hype that can\'t live up to the scrutiny and long-term use?'

[10:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Noon\'s system is capturing more than just carbon. They have multiple partners and funding sources like NASA, the NSF, and the California Energy Commission. In other words, this technology is gaining the attention and support of some pretty'

[10:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'big names. Here\'s my take. Noon\'s new ultra-long duration energy storage system could help pave the way for a fossil fuel-independent future if it can live up to the promises and truly scale at a low cost. Considering'

[10:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this tech was developed by a NASA engineer turned green energy guru, I am hopeful. Graves told Latitude Media in a January 2026 interview, "They\'ve gotten to a technology readiness level that is'

[10:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'high enough to start producing units on a production line. So, that\'s [music] our next big goal." I\'ll definitely be keeping a close eye on them to see what happens next. My last thought, somewhere out there a biology teacher is feeling very vindicated right now.'

